Source: https://github.com/unbraind/pm-review/pull/1#discussion_r4248463348

**🩺 Stability & Availability** | **🟠 Major** | **⚡ Quick win**

**Keep the event loop alive in the timeout test.**

`createGitHubTransport` uses `AbortSignal.timeout(timeoutMs)`. Node.js runs that timer as unref'd, so the timer does not keep the process alive. The `stuck` fetch has no other active handle. The event loop therefore empties before the abort fires. `node:test` then cancels the test with "Promise resolution remained pending" and also cancels the seven tests that follow. CI fails at `npm run coverage`.

Production is not affected, because a real `fetch` keeps an open socket. Fix the test so that it holds a ref'd handle until the abort fires.

<details>
<summary>💚 Proposed fix</summary>

```diff
--- "a/test/github.test.ts"
+++ "b/test/github.test.ts"
@@ -559,10 +559,15 @@
   await rejects(syntheticTransport([gql({ resolveReviewThread: null })]).resolve('RT_1'), /\(resolveReviewThread payload\)/);
 });

 test('bounds every request with the configured timeout', () => {
   const stuck: FetchLike = (_url, init) => new Promise((_resolve, reject) => {
-    init.signal.addEventListener('abort', () => reject(new Error('request timed out')), { once: true });
+    // AbortSignal.timeout is unref'd; hold a ref'd handle like a real open socket would.
+    const keepAlive = setTimeout(() => undefined, 5_000);
+    init.signal.addEventListener('abort', () => {
+      clearTimeout(keepAlive);
+      reject(new Error('request timed out'));
+    }, { once: true });
   });
   const transport = createGitHubTransport({ fetch: stuck, token: TOKEN, timeoutMs: 10 });
   return rejects(transport.viewer(), /request timed out/);
 });
```
</details>

